    <string name="about_data_source_title">NASA Exoplanet Archive</string>
    <string name="about_data_source_description">Every planet and star shown in this app comes from one public source: the NASA Exoplanet Archive, operated by Caltech under contract with NASA. It is the global reference for confirmed exoplanets, combining results from missions like Kepler, K2, and TESS with major ground-based surveys. The app ships with a recent snapshot of the archive and can refresh it on demand from the archive\'s public service.</string>
    <string name="about_verification_title">How Planets Are Confirmed</string>
    <string name="about_verification_description">This app does not verify, edit, or generate any of this data. Every planet listed was confirmed by professional astronomers through the standard scientific process — usually multiple independent observations that establish the planet exists and measure properties like its mass, radius, and orbit — before being published in the archive.</string>
    <string name="about_ml_title">On-Device Estimates</string>
    <string name="about_ml_description">The habitability scores and planet classifications are produced by a small machine-learning model that runs entirely on your device, using the measured features from the archive. They are the app\'s own approximate, educational estimates — not official assessments from NASA or any observatory — and are not evidence that a planet is truly habitable.</string>
    <string name="about_affiliation_title">Not Affiliated with NASA</string>
    <string name="about_affiliation_description">Exoplanet Hunter is an independent, unofficial project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by NASA, Caltech, or IPAC. Those organisations are credited only as the source of the public data the app displays, as the archive\'s usage policy requires.</string>
